    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d36">

                <pb facs="0095" n="69" xml:id="pg69"/>

                <head>36. Memorandum of Telephone Conversation Between the Secretary of State and
                    the Assistant Secretary of State for Far Eastern Affairs (Harriman)<anchor n="1" xml:id="d36-f1"/></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#italic"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            type="conversation-or-meeting-date" when="1963-08-31T10:15:00-04:00"><hi
                                rendition="#italic">August 31, 1963—10:15
                        a.m.</hi></date></dateline></opener>
                <p rendition="#center">TELEPHONE CALL FROM AMB.
                        HARRIMAN</p>
                <p>Amb. Harriman said he would come back to Washington if need be. He
                    did not think events entirely surprising (Vietnam). He thought we should go back
                    to the program we recommended a week ago,<anchor n="2" xml:id="d36-f2"/> and
                    have it out. He thought we should tell Diem what we were going to do and stick to it. We had gone
                    through the same thing with Phoumi.<anchor n="3" xml:id="d36-f3"/>
                    Harriman asked if the Sec had seen
                        Kattenburg. The Sec said he had but
                    that Kattenburg’s report<anchor n="4" xml:id="d36-f4"/> was already out of date
                    in some important respects.</p>
                <p>The Sec. said either he or Mr. Hilsman
                    would be in touch with Amb. Harriman later in the day.</p>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d36-f1" n="1" type="source" xml:id="d36-f1-content">
                <p>Source: Department of State, Rusk Files:
                    Lot 72 D 192, Telephone Conversations. Transcribed by Mildred Asbjornson.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d36-f2" n="2" xml:id="d36-f2-content">
                <p>Apparent reference to telegram 243 to Saigon, <ref target="frus1961-63v03#pg_628"
                        >vol. III, p. 628</ref>.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d36-f3" n="3" xml:id="d36-f3-content">
                <p>Reference is to Harriman’s efforts to
                    convince Phoumi Nosovan to cooperate with U.S. support of a neutralized
                    Laos.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d36-f4" n="4" xml:id="d36-f4-content">
                <p>Rusk apparently is referring to
                    Kattenburg’s point of view, set forth in the memorandum of conversation <ref
                        target="#d37"><hi rendition="#italic">infra</hi></ref>.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
